nd accelerated paradigm of discovery."}, {"id": "https://openalex.org/W4385804359", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w31558", "title": "Artificial Intelligence and Scientific Discovery: A Model of Prioritized Search", "publication_year": 2023, "type": "report", "cited_by_count": 15, "open_access": {"is_oa": true, "oa_status": "green", "oa_url": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w31558", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "doi:10.3386/w31558", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w31558", "is_oa": true, "landing_page_url": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w31558", "pdf_url": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w31558", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S2809516038", "display_name": "National Bureau of Economic Research", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": null, "host_organization_name": null, "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/P4322614461"], "host_organization_lineage_names": ["National Bureau of Economic Research"], "type": "repository"}, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "National Bureau of Economic Research", "raw_type": "report"}, "abstract": "We model a key step in the innovation process, hypothesis generation, as the making of predictions over a vast combinatorial space.Traditionally, scientists and innovators use theory or intuition to guide their search.Increasingly, however, they use artificial intelligence (AI) instead.We model innovation as resulting from sequential search over a combinatorial design space, where the prioritization of costly tests is achieved using a predictive model.We represent the ranked output of the predictive model in the form of a hazard function.We then use discrete survival analysis to obtain the main innovation outcomes of interest -the probability of innovation, expected search duration, and expected profit.We describe conditions 